  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Esho Shikhi (&#x201c;Come and Learn&#x201d;)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Bangladesh):  Bangladesh has made remarkable progress over the past decade in expanding access to education by achieving near universal net primary enrollment, with over 98 percent of primary school age children enrolled in school. However, access to schools and retention remain low in crisis affected areas such as flood-prone regions, and in marginalized and vulnerable communities across the country. The situation is even worse for girls in areas of conflict, particularly the host communities of Cox&apos;s Bazar. The activity addresses two USAID Education Policy priorities: 1) increase access to safe, quality education that is relevant and promotes social well-being, especially for marginalized and vulnerable children, and 2) develop the early grade reading and literacy skills necessary for children&apos;s future learning and success. With FY 2024 funds, USAID&apos;s Esho Shikhi (Come and Learn) activity will continue to improve the learning outcomes of Bangladeshi children in schools in crisis-affected and marginalized communities across the country, including girls in areas of conflict. The activity will provide training to trainers, teachers, and headteachers to improve the quality of Bangla literacy acquisition for 2.3 million children. It will also enhance the ability of education officials and other stakeholders at district and sub-district levels to plan, manage, and oversee quality education programs and increase the capacity of school communities to mitigate and manage the effects of shocks and stressors on the education system. It will provide technical assistance, awareness-building campaigns, training for community members, and teaching-learning materials featuring the establishment of classroom-based library/reading corners.Funding will contribute to the Basic Education ($2,549,082) earmark with Girls in Areas of Conflict ($2,130,570) and Private Sector Engagement ($2,039) attributions.</narrative>
  </description>
